# nod, bounce and toeLook, measured in the browser through the real engine,
# before and after the facing term. 2026-09-10.
#
# The page is a dev-server session, so this file is the only record of these
# numbers, the same reason bow-0910-browser.log and armrest-0909-browser.log
# exist.
#
#   npm run dev
#   http://localhost:5173/scripts/avatar/live-preview.html?mikadebug=1
#   ?mikadebug=1 exposes window.__mikaVrm and window.__mikaHandle.
#   Playwright drove both passes. 143 to 157 frames in each 2.6s window.
#
# Body: public/avatar/mika-milfy-12.vrm, the milfy look the site serves, which
#   reports VRM metaVersion 0 and carries scene.rotation.y = pi from
#   VRMUtils.rotateVRM0. facingSign('0') is -1, so a correct pitch term here is
#   NEGATIVE.
#
# WHAT IS READ, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE EYE. bow-0910-browser.log reads the eye's
# world position and needs a 40-sample baseline to clear the idle floor, because
# breathing and weight shift move her eyes by tens of millimetres. Two of the
# three gestures here are too small for that: nod's whole pitch is 0.14 rad,
# worth about 8mm of eye swing against a floor measured below at 16mm.
#
# So this pass reads the gesture term itself, which the engine hands back
# exactly. With no clip running it writes, on the same frame,
#
#     head.rotation.x = pitch * 0.7 + OFF.hp
#     neck.rotation.x = pitch * 0.3
#
# and `pitch` is the mode-driven gaze, the only other writer. Two bones, two
# unknowns:
#
#     OFF.hp = head.rotation.x - (7 / 3) * neck.rotation.x
#
# `OFF.sx` needs no algebra at all: spine.rotation.x = OFF.sx outright.
#
# THE CONTROL RUN PROVES THE SEPARATION. `__none__` is the same 2.6s window with
# no gesture clicked. Over 157 frames the recovered hp and sx are 0.000 and
# 0.000, not "small": the gaze cancels exactly. The eye position in the same
# control window swings 16mm in Z and 5mm in Y, which is the floor the eye
# reading would have had to clear.
#
# The mode is `listening`, not `idle`, for the length of both passes. In idle
# the engine fires an unprompted act or a motion clip every 2.5 to 4 seconds and
# would have landed one on top of a gesture under measurement; with mode not
# idle it skips that block entirely and holds the timer at 4s or more.
#
# BEFORE is the shipped code with the three `fwd *` factors taken back out, on
# the same dev server, confirmed by curl on the served module before the pass.
# AFTER is the code as committed, confirmed the same way.
#
#   gesture   quantity        BEFORE            AFTER
#   nod       peak hp        +0.108 @  256ms   -0.108 @  258ms
#             first beat     +0.030 @   88ms   -0.030 @   91ms
#   bounce    peak hp        +0.050 @  587ms   -0.050 @  600ms
#             peak sx        +0.050 @  587ms   -0.050 @  600ms
#             first beat     +0.034 @  487ms   -0.034 @  500ms
#   toeLook   peak hp        +0.300 @ 1000ms   -0.300 @  994ms
#             first beat     +0.031 @   66ms   -0.032 @   64ms
#   __none__  peak hp         0.000             0.000
#             peak sx         0.000             0.000
#
# Opposite signs on all nine gesture rows, and the two `__none__` rows read
# 0.000 in both passes, which is what a control is for. Eight of the nine agree
# in magnitude to three decimals; the ninth is toeLook's first beat, +0.031
# against -0.032, because
# "first frame past 0.03 rad" lands on a different frame in the two passes and
# the envelope is still climbing there. On a 0.x body a negative head pitch is
# chin DOWN: the offline probe (pitch-0910.log) has hp = -0.14 carrying this
# family's eye 7.67mm toward her face and 4.70mm down.
#
# nod is the one whose SIZE does not change, only its phase, so its evidence is
# the "first beat" row: the first frame whose recovered hp clears 0.03 rad. It
# was positive, and a positive pitch on a 0.x body is chin UP. She answered
# "yes" by lifting her chin first.
#
# WHAT THE EYE SAW ANYWAY, for the two that clear the floor. At the toeLook peak
# her eye moved 8mm UP before and 16mm DOWN after, against a control floor of
# 5mm in Y. Before the fix the gesture sent her gaze target down and her head
# up at the same time.
#
== the screenshots, each bound to the frame it was read on ==
#   file                             shot at   recovered hp   argmax    ink
#   pitch-0910-toelook-before.png     997.3ms      +0.300     997.3ms   0.203
#   pitch-0910-toelook-after.png      996.8ms      -0.300     996.8ms   0.200
#
# Both are the argmax of their own trace, not a nominal 990ms: the capture
# records elapsed time and the recovered hp every frame, reads the canvas on the
# first frame at or past 990ms, and afterwards confirms that frame carried the
# largest |hp| of the whole trace. It did, in both.
#
# `ink` is the fraction of a 160px-wide copy of the FULL canvas that is not
# black, taken in the same frame. It guards the one silent failure: a canvas
# read on the wrong side of a paint comes back blank, and a blank PNG still
# looks like a successful screenshot. 0.203 and 0.200 are the character; a
# cleared buffer reads 0. The buffer is there to be read because
# avatarGuideEngine sets preserveDrawingBuffer from `?mikadebug=1` and nothing
# else (avatarGuideEngine.ts, the `capturable` const); an earlier draft of this
# paragraph said `?n=1`, which nothing in src/ or scripts/ reads.
#
# THE PAIR ARE TWO POSES, checked the way bow-0910.md had to be after both
# reviewers caught a pair that were not.
#
# THE SAMPLE IS THE TWO COMMITTED FILES, pitch-0910-toelook-before.png and
# -after.png, 944x856 each, both cropped from the 2140x1456 canvas with ONE
# shared box (608,344)-(1552,1200). A first draft of this block quoted the same
# four measures taken on the uncropped 2140x1456 frames, which are not committed,
# so its centroid of 1018.4 was not even a row that exists in the artifact beside
# it. Foreground is a pixel whose RGB differs from the corner background by more
# than 12 on the channel mean:
#
#   foreground pixels      409,484 before against 413,592 after
#   topmost foreground row      34 against 50, so 16px apart
#   foreground centroid, Y  538.39 against 523.75, so 14.65px apart
#   mask XOR                                            56,052px
#   mask XOR over mean foreground                          13.6%
#
# No count is carried across from the pair that failed review on 09-10: those
# were a different crop of a different body pose and their counts are not
# comparable to these. What that pair was is the point: two near-idle frames,
# 0.6px of centroid apart on their own crop (bow-0910-browser.log). These two
# are not that.
#
# NOT MEASURED HERE: the other seven gestures. Which ones read the facing is
# settled by the diff and pinned by avatarPitch.test.ts, whose VERSION_BLIND
# block asserts that `wiggle`, `tilt`, `glance`, `swayStep`, `hipTwist` and
# `leanBack` return identical offsets at both signs of `fwd`. A browser sweep
# would be a weaker version of that.
